::GEO_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
A figure is a straight path with arrows on both ends. Which description is most precise?
{
=A. A line#Correct. A line is a straight path that extends forever in both directions, and arrows on both ends show that extension.
~B. A segment#A segment has two endpoints. Arrows on both ends show the figure does not stop at endpoints.
~C. A ray#A ray has one endpoint and extends in one direction. This figure extends in both directions.
~D. An angle#An angle is formed by two rays with a common endpoint. The description gives one straight path.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
A figure has endpoints A and B. Which description matches the definition?
{
~A. Line AB#A line extends forever in both directions and does not have endpoints.
~B. Ray AB#A ray has one endpoint and extends in one direction, so two endpoints do not match the definition.
=C. Segment AB#Correct. A segment is a part of a line with two endpoints, so endpoints A and B identify segment AB.
~D. Angle AB#An angle is formed by two rays with a common endpoint. Two letters alone do not name a standard angle here.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which statement is most precise?
{
~A. There is a corner at B.#This is informal and does not name the rays or the angle.
=B. Rays BA and BC form angle ABC.#Correct. This names the two rays and the angle they form.
~C. The shape bends at B.#This is vague and does not use precise Geometry language.
~D. B is somewhere in the figure.#This does not describe the geometric relationship clearly.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
A student says a figure is a ray because it looks short. What is the best correction?
{
~A. A ray is always the shortest object in a diagram.#Length in a drawing does not define a ray. A ray is defined by one endpoint and extension in one direction.
~B. A ray must have two endpoints.#This confuses a ray with a segment. A ray has one endpoint.
~C. Any short figure is a segment.#Appearance is not enough evidence. The defining features are endpoints and arrows.
=D. Use endpoints and arrows, not appearance, to identify the object.#Correct. Definitions and markings provide evidence; appearance alone is not proof.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which phrase best describes evidence in a Geometry explanation?
{
~A. A guess based on what the diagram looks like#A guess is not evidence. Geometry explanations need support from definitions, labels, markings, or given information.
=B. Information that supports a conclusion#Correct. Evidence supports a conclusion using definitions, labels, markings, or given statements.
~C. A word that sounds mathematical#Mathematical vocabulary helps only when it is used accurately and supported by meaning.
~D. A conclusion without a reason#A conclusion needs evidence. A statement without a reason does not explain the Geometry.
}

